    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Direct mechanistic evidence**\n\n* **CFTR loss → dysbiosis (animal)** – Whole‑body and tissue‑specific Cftr knockout mice show altered gut communities. Global Cftr deletion changes microbial composition in both small and large intestine, with immune‑cell loss driving small‑intestinal dysbiosis and epithelial loss driving large‑intestinal shifts【PMID:36289287】. An intestinal‑epithelial Cftr KO alone produces marked beta‑diversity changes and enrichment of Proteobacteria, Enterobacteriaceae and Clostridia【PMID:37546931】.  \n\n* **Dysbiosis → inflammation (animal)** – In Cftr‑KO mice, the dysbiotic expansion of Enterobacteriaceae/Enterococcus accompanies increased intestinal permeability and peri‑portal liver inflammation; treatment with non‑absorbable antibiotics restores barrier function and reduces liver inflammation, implicating the dysbiotic microbiota as a driver【PMID:38554847】.  \n\n* **CFTR modulation → concurrent microbiota and inflammation changes (human)** – Ivacaftor therapy in CF patients raises Akkermansia and lowers Enterobacteriaceae while significantly decreasing fecal calprotectin; the correlation between reduced pro‑inflammatory taxa and lower inflammation supports a causal link between dysbiosis correction and inflammation resolution【PMID:30546102】.  \n\n**Associative evidence supporting the path**\n\n* Human and mouse studies consistently report that CF‑related gut dysbiosis is characterized by reduced diversity and enrichment of pro‑inflammatory taxa (Proteobacteria, Enterobacteriaceae) together with elevated fecal calprotectin or mucosal cytokines【PMID:24548777】【PMID:37546931】.  \n\n* Systematic reviews note the co‑occurrence of dysbiosis and local/systemic inflammation in CF, though direct mechanistic experiments are limited【PMID:36600119】.  \n\n**Overall inference**\n\nThe combined animal knockout data (CFTR loss → dysbiosis) and interventional studies (dysbiosis → reduced inflammation) provide a stepwise mechanistic chain, while human correlative data reinforce the relevance to patients. No single experiment has simultaneously demonstrated that restoring a normal microbiota fully rescues CFTR‑deficiency‑induced inflammation, leaving the complete causal pathway an open hypothesis.  \n\n**Conclusion** – Multiple animal models and a human CFTR‑modulator trial substantiate the mechanistic route: CFTR deficiency creates gut dysbiosis, which in turn promotes intestinal (and hepatic) inflammation.\n\nGraph-derived candidate insights\n- PATH 9ed6292d75395474 — GRAPH-INCREMENTAL CANDIDATE; multi-hop inference; HOP 1: cftr causes cystic fibrosis [PMID: 36289287]; HOP 2: cystic fibrosis leads_to inflammation [PMID: 33201261].\n- PATH 57416f2f7b9449cc — GRAPH-CONFIRMED; multi-hop inference; HOP 1: cftr induces dysbiosis [PMID: 37546931]; HOP 2: dysbiosis associated_with inflammation [PMID: 36600119].\n- PATH daf58598d61d93bf — GRAPH-CONFIRMED; direct graph evidence; HOP 1: cftr induces inflammation [PMID: 37546931]."
